# .env.example — TEMPLATE ONLY. PLACEHOLDERS, NEVER REAL VALUES.
#
# This is the one env file in the tree that is NOT gitignored (.gitignore: `.env.*`
# then `!.env.example`). NOTE: `.env.deploy.example` WOULD be ignored by that rule —
# hence this exact filename. It is also on check-secrets' ALLOW list, which means the
# secret scanner does NOT protect this file. Putting a real value here commits it.
#
# COPY, NEVER EDIT IN PLACE:
#   cp .env.example .env.migrate   # only DATABASE_URL is needed to migrate
#   cp .env.example .env.deploy    # the preflight set
# Both copies are gitignored. SOURCE them, never READ them (CLAUDE.md; the
# secret-file-guard hook enforces it):
#   set -a; . ./.env.migrate; set +a; pnpm db:migrate
# Never `cat`/`echo` one — that puts the secret into an agent's context.
#
# Runtime secrets for the deployed Edge Functions do NOT come from this file. They
# are set once per project with `supabase secrets set KEY=…`; the shims read them
# through Deno.env (supabase/functions/_shared/pool.ts, packages/functions/src/auth/env.ts).
#
# ─── ROLE / IDENTITY IS THE WHOLE POINT ──────────────────────────────────────
# Two of these are Postgres connection strings whose ROLE *is* a privilege boundary.
# There is no runtime check that they are right: makeServiceExecutor issues no
# `SET LOCAL ROLE`, so whatever identity SUPABASE_DB_SERVICE_URL carries is the
# authority the money write runs with. Getting either wrong is silent until a real
# user or a real purchase hits it. Preflight A.1 is what makes it loud instead:
#   pnpm -w exec vitest run --project integration packages/functions/test/preflight.integration.test.ts
# See docs/DEPLOY-RUNBOOK.md.


# ══ 1. RUNTIME — the 4 vars the deployed Edge Functions require ══════════════

# Postgres connection string for the APP role. Used by all 11 user-JWT routes
# (every supabase/functions/*/index.ts except revenuecat-webhook).
# REQUIRED IDENTITY: a plain login role that is GRANTed `app_user` and is NOT
#   RLS-exempt. makePgExecutor runs `SET LOCAL ROLE app_user` + binds the verified
#   JWT sub per transaction, so RLS confines every row to the caller.
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG:
#   · Too weak (cannot SET ROLE app_user) → every user-JWT route 500s. Total outage.
#   · TOO STRONG (service_role / superuser / BYPASSRLS) → RLS no longer confines
#     anything: cross-tenant reads become possible and ANY authenticated user can
#     write public.subscriptions, i.e. mint their own entitlement. The paywall is
#     bypassable and the tenancy guarantee is gone. Caught by preflight A.1c.
#   · Points at a different database than SUPABASE_DB_SERVICE_URL → entitlement is
#     written to one DB and read from another; the customer pays and stays locked
#     out. Caught by preflight A.1b.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://REPLACE_ROLE:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Postgres connection string for the SERVICE role. Used by revenuecat-webhook ONLY
# (supabase/functions/revenuecat-webhook/index.ts) — the sole sanctioned RLS-bypass
# seam in the codebase.
# REQUIRED IDENTITY: the genuinely RLS-exempt `service_role` (or a BYPASSRLS role
#   with INSERT/UPDATE on public.subscriptions and public.webhook_events).
#   makeServiceExecutor issues NO `SET LOCAL ROLE`, so this pool's OWN identity is
#   the privilege boundary. app_user has SELECT-only on subscriptions (0008) and no
#   access at all to webhook_events (0009).
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG (LAUNCH-READINESS §6.2 — the most dangerous misconfig):
#   every real purchase event raises Postgres 42501, the webhook 500s, RevenueCat
#   retries forever, entitlement NEVER flips, and a PAYING customer stays locked out
#   of kind=full. The safety property (a client cannot mint entitlement) becomes the
#   day-1 outage. Caught by preflight A.1a.
# NEVER point a user-JWT route at this string.
SUPABASE_DB_SERVICE_URL=postgres://REPLACE_SERVICE_ROLE:REPLACE_PASSWORD@REPLACE_HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# The asymmetric JWKS endpoint withAuth verifies every user JWT against
# (packages/functions/src/auth/withAuth.ts:51 — createRemoteJWKSet).
# REQUIRED IDENTITY: not a credential at all — a public URL, and it must be the
#   ASYMMETRIC JWKS of this project. Asymmetric is the point: the private key never
#   leaves the auth provider, so there is no shared secret to leak.
#   Typically https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG: unreachable, mistyped, or belonging to a DIFFERENT project
#   → jwtVerify fails → EVERY one of the 11 user-JWT routes returns 401. The app is
#   entirely unusable while still looking deployed. Caught by preflight A.2.
JWKS_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json

# The RevenueCat webhook shared secret. revenuecat-webhook compares the inbound
# Authorization header against this in constant time
# (packages/functions/src/billing/revenuecat-webhook.ts:144).
# REQUIRED IDENTITY: a high-entropy random string, byte-identical to the
#   Authorization header value configured in the RevenueCat dashboard. It is the
#   ONLY thing authenticating the money path — there is no JWT on a webhook request.
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG:
#   · Mismatched → every real event 401s; entitlement never flips (same customer-
#     facing outcome as a bad SUPABASE_DB_SERVICE_URL, different cause).
#   · Leaked/guessable → anyone who can reach the URL can forge purchase events and
#     grant themselves entitlement. Rotate on both sides simultaneously.
REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_STRING


# ══ 2. PREFLIGHT-ONLY — never set on the deployed project ════════════════════
# Consumed by packages/functions/test/preflight.integration.test.ts. Absent → those
# checks SKIP with a loud banner (CI stays green and honest). Reuses DATABASE_URL,
# SUPABASE_DB_SERVICE_URL and JWKS_URL from section 1, so put both sections in the
# same .env.deploy copy.

# Master opt-in AND the project label echoed in every failure message. Required by
# every env-gated preflight check, so a stale exported DATABASE_URL can never make
# preflight fire against the wrong database by accident.
PREFLIGHT_PROJECT_REF=REPLACE_PROJECT_REF

# Base URL the Edge Functions are served under. Preflight A.4 hits every route here
# with a bad token and requires withAuth's own 401 envelope — a different shape
# proves the Supabase gateway rejected the request first (verify_jwt left ON), which
# would 401 every REAL request too.
PREFLIGHT_FUNCTIONS_BASE_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co/functions/v1

# Project URL + anon key for the Storage REST probe (preflight B.1).
# The anon key is public-by-design (RLS is the real control) — but it is still a
# JWT-shaped string, so a REAL one pasted below is committed AND invisible to
# check-secrets, which allowlists this file. Keep the placeholder.
PREFLIGHT_SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
PREFLIGHT_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_ANON_KEY

# Two access tokens for two DIFFERENT throwaway test users, used by preflight B.1 to
# prove Storage RLS binds the path prefix to the requester's sub on the `originals`
# and `cutouts` buckets — user A must be refused read AND write under user B's
# prefix (LAUNCH-READINESS §6.5 / docs/06 §6). These are short-lived user JWTs, not
# service keys; obtain them by signing in as each test user. Mint fresh ones per
# preflight run rather than storing them.
# PREFLIGHT_USER_A_JWT IS ALSO REQUIRED BY A.2b, not just B.1. A.2b drives the
#   PRODUCTION makeJwksVerifier against a real user token, and it is the ONLY check
#   that catches a JWKS_URL belonging to a DIFFERENT project — A.2a passes against any
#   live JWKS endpoint, so without this var the wrong-project trap is unguarded (every
#   real request would 401). Setting PREFLIGHT_PROJECT_REF + JWKS_URL but not this is a
#   partial env: preflight then names A.2b in the SKIPPED banner. Set it.
# BLAST RADIUS IF THESE ARE THE SAME USER: B.1 becomes a tautology (a "cross-prefix"
# probe against your own prefix). The test asserts subA !== subB and fails loudly.
PREFLIGHT_USER_A_JWT=REPLACE_WITH_TEST_USER_A_ACCESS_TOKEN
PREFLIGHT_USER_B_JWT=REPLACE_WITH_TEST_USER_B_ACCESS_TOKEN


# ══ 3. PROVIDER KEYS — REQUIRED, the parse loop is dead without them ═════════
# The real adapters ARE wired now (packages/functions/src/adapters/); parse-photo is
# no longer bound to unwiredPorts. requireEnv throws on the first provider call when
# a key is unset, so parse-photo returns 502 parse_provider_failed for EVERY user
# until both are set — the core loop (camera roll → cutout → reveal → paywall) is
# inert. Set these with `supabase secrets set`, not in a committed file.
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG: 502 on every parse. There is no product until these work.
OPENAI_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_OPENAI_KEY
PHOTOROOM_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_PHOTOROOM_KEY

# ── LLM efficiency knobs (OPTIONAL — sane defaults; leave unset to start) ──
# OPENAI_VISION_IMAGE_DETAIL: 'low' | 'high' | 'auto'. UNSET = the model's `auto`
# (current behaviour). 'low' fixes the image to a small ~85-token tile — the single
# biggest per-call COST lever — BUT it can degrade `material`/fine-`pattern` accuracy,
# which is the product's make-or-break metric. DO NOT set 'low' until the Tier-1
# bench-scan corpus (docs/05) proves accuracy holds; it is a corpus-gated decision.
# OPENAI_VISION_IMAGE_DETAIL=auto
#
# Output-token cap for the vision call. Default 400 — generous vs the ~80-token JSON
# this schema produces, so a valid response never truncates while a runaway is bounded.
# (Wired in the adapter; env override not read today — injected in tests.)

# The cutout writer uploads bytes to the private `cutouts` bucket over Storage's
# HTTP API under the CALLER'S own JWT (never service_role — a bypassing key would
# make a wrong path succeed and void migration 0013's cross-user control). It reads
# the project URL + anon key at runtime, so these are RUNTIME vars, not test-only.
# BLAST RADIUS IF WRONG/ABSENT: every cutout write fails → 502 on every parse.
SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_ANON_KEY


# ══ 4. MOBILE (Expo) — a separate file, listed for completeness ══════════════
# Metro inlines EXPO_PUBLIC_* at bundle time (packages/mobile/src/api/config.ts).
# These belong in the mobile app's own .env / EAS env, not in .env.deploy.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_ANON_KEY
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FUNCTIONS_BASE_URL=https://REPLACE_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co/functions/v1
